WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.650
The human eye has three types of cells that detect color.

2
00:00:03.650 --> 00:00:07.460
A shrimp living on coral reefs has twelve.

3
00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:12.710
For years, the internet claimed that this animal sees colors we cannot even imagine.

4
00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:18.120
Then someone actually gave the shrimp a color test, and the result surprised everyone.

5
00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:33.170
On the shallow coral reefs of Indonesia, two eyes peer out from the mouth of a small burrow dug into the sand.

6
00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:38.100
Each eye sits at the tip of a stalk and swivels independently of the other.

7
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:43.830
One looks upward while the other drifts to the side, and then both lock onto the same spot at once.

8
00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:48.280
Their owner is a mantis shrimp, no more than a few centimeters long.

9
00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:53.210
Despite its name, it is not a close relative of the shrimp we eat.

10
00:00:53.210 --> 00:01:00.380
It belongs to a separate order of crustaceans called the stomatopods, an order with more than four hundred species.

11
00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:08.030
Its fame usually comes from its punch: some species fire their front appendages so fast that they can shatter snail shells.

12
00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:12.640
But what has truly occupied scientists for many years is not the punch.

13
00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:15.970
It is the eyes at the ends of those stalks.

14
00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:18.980
First, let's look at our own eyes.

15
00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:23.270
The human retina contains three types of cone cells for color.

16
00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:33.160
One is most sensitive to short wavelengths, that is, to blue; one to medium wavelengths, that is, to green; and one to long wavelengths, the region close to red.

17
00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:38.090
The millions of shades we see arise from comparing the signals these three cells send.

18
00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:42.860
In dogs, the number is two; in many birds, four.

19
00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:50.350
But in the species of mantis shrimp that have been studied, the number of receptor types devoted to color vision rises to twelve.

20
00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:56.400
Some of these capture ultraviolet light, the part of the spectrum beyond violet that we cannot see at all.

21
00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:03.410
Counting the cells dedicated to polarized light as well, the total number of receptor types reaches around sixteen.

22
00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:07.540
It is one of the most crowded visual toolkits known in the animal kingdom.

23
00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:12.630
Something else is just as striking as the numbers: the architecture of the eye.

24
00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:18.840
Like an insect's, the mantis shrimp's eye is a compound eye made up of thousands of tiny units.

25
00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:22.170
But this eye is divided into three regions.

26
00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:29.820
There is a hemisphere on top, a hemisphere below, and running straight through the middle, a narrow strip made up of six rows of units.

27
00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:32.830
Researchers call this strip the midband.

28
00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:37.920
The upper and lower hemispheres mainly handle shape, motion and brightness.

29
00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:43.010
Color, on the other hand, is almost entirely the job of this thin midband.

30
00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:51.940
In the units of the band's first four rows, receptors are stacked one on top of another along the path the light travels, with colored filters between them.

31
00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:57.190
These filters absorb part of the light and pass the rest down to the cell beneath.

32
00:02:57.190 --> 00:03:02.120
In this way, each cell comes to see a fairly narrow slice of the spectrum.

33
00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:09.130
The last two rows deal not with color but with the direction in which light vibrates, that is, with polarization.

34
00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:12.300
This structure has an interesting consequence.

35
00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:22.670
Because the upper hemisphere, the lower hemisphere and the midband can all look at the same point from different angles, a single eye may not need a second eye to judge how far away an object is.

36
00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:26.960
We work out depth from the difference between the images our two eyes see.

37
00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:32.850
The mantis shrimp, so to speak, carries three separate views in each eye.

38
00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:38.420
Taken together, the two eyes make for an extraordinarily flexible surveillance system.

39
00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:42.870
Anyone looking at such equipment has the same thought.

40
00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:50.360
If the world we see with three receptors is this colorful, the world seen with twelve must be unimaginably rich in detail.

41
00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:54.330
In popular writing, this idea was often exaggerated.

42
00:03:54.330 --> 00:04:01.660
It was said that the mantis shrimp sees colors we cannot even imagine, and that it catches even the slightest difference between shades.

43
00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:06.750
The logic seemed simple: more receptors, finer discrimination.

44
00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:12.000
But this was an assumption, and the way to test an assumption was to ask the animal itself.

45
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:19.970
At the University of Queensland in Australia, a team that included Hanne Thoen and Justin Marshall decided to do exactly that.

46
00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:25.700
The star of the experiment was a small species of mantis shrimp called Haptosquilla trispinosa.

47
00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:32.230
The question was clear: how close can two colors be before this animal can no longer tell them apart?

48
00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:36.680
Asking a crustacean a question about color is not easy.

49
00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:40.330
The team took advantage of the animal's natural behavior.

50
00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:45.100
Mantis shrimp are quick learners, capable of learning to strike at a target.

51
00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:51.950
The researchers placed optical fibers in the aquarium, each glowing at its tip with light of a specific wavelength.

52
00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:57.520
Whenever the shrimp went for the light of the correct color, it received food as a reward.

53
00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:01.490
Once this training was complete, the real test began.

54
00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:08.180
The animal was shown the color it had learned on one side and, at the same time, a similar color on the other.

55
00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:10.390
Could the shrimp pick the right one?

56
00:05:10.390 --> 00:05:14.200
Here, the idea of wavelength needs a little unpacking.

57
00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:19.290
The light our eyes can see spans roughly four hundred to seven hundred nanometers.

58
00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:22.940
A nanometer is one billionth of a meter.

59
00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:34.270
Around four hundred fifty looks blue to us, around five hundred thirty green, around five hundred eighty yellow, a little above six hundred orange, and six hundred fifty and beyond red.

60
00:05:34.270 --> 00:05:40.960
In some regions of the spectrum, the human eye can distinguish two lights that differ by just one to four nanometers.

61
00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:47.810
In the transition between yellow and orange, we can pick up even shifts so small they are barely noticeable.

62
00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:54.340
When the results were published in the journal Science in twenty fourteen, they turned expectations upside down.

63
00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:58.790
The mantis shrimp did well when the difference between the two lights was large.

64
00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:02.760
But as the difference shrank, their consistency collapsed.

65
00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:14.250
The exact threshold varied across different parts of the spectrum, but once the gap between two wavelengths fell below roughly twelve to twenty-five nanometers, the animal could no longer reliably make the right choice.

66
00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:23.820
In other words, two shades that we tell apart with ease, say an orange leaning toward yellow and a yellow leaning toward orange, were often one and the same to this creature.

67
00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:28.910
To grasp just how surprising this was, consider the inverse relationship.

68
00:06:28.910 --> 00:06:34.480
An eye with three receptors was making color distinctions many times finer than an eye with twelve.

69
00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:39.730
It was as if a three-stringed instrument could play subtler notes than a twelve-stringed one.

70
00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:41.940
Something was off.

71
00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:50.070
Either the experiment was failing to measure the animal's true ability, or our assumptions about color vision simply did not apply to this creature.

72
00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:58.520
The team leaned toward the second explanation, and to understand it, we need to look at how the human eye works once more, but this time from the brain's side.

73
00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:02.970
Our three cone cells do not tell us colors on their own.

74
00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:10.300
For instance, just because the cone sensitive to long wavelengths sends a strong signal, our brain does not immediately say red.

75
00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:15.390
Our nervous system subtracts the cones' signals from one another and compares them.

76
00:07:15.390 --> 00:07:20.000
It builds opposing channels, such as red against green and blue against yellow.

77
00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:25.410
What determines a color is the subtle ratio between the receptors' signals.

78
00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:34.020
This method of comparison is extraordinarily sensitive, because even a tiny shift in the ratio between two receptors is perceived as a new shade.

79
00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:37.830
The price, however, is intensive neural processing.

80
00:07:37.830 --> 00:07:43.720
In our retina and our brain, layer upon layer of nerve-cell circuitry is devoted to color.

81
00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:47.690
Now imagine applying the same method to twelve receptors.

82
00:07:47.690 --> 00:07:53.580
Comparing each of the twelve receptors with all the others would mean building dozens of opposing channels.

83
00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:58.350
That would place an enormous burden on the nervous system of a small crustacean.

84
00:07:58.350 --> 00:08:06.160
What's more, the weak discriminating power that Thoen and her colleagues measured suggested that this kind of comparison was not happening at all.

85
00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:12.050
Had it been, receptors covering the spectrum so densely should have delivered very fine discrimination.

86
00:08:12.050 --> 00:08:13.780
They did not.

87
00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:17.750
So the shrimp must have been recognizing color some other way.

88
00:08:17.750 --> 00:08:26.200
The idea the team proposed was this: the mantis shrimp may see colors not by comparing them with one another, but by recognizing them directly.

89
00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:28.890
Let's think about it through an analogy.

90
00:08:28.890 --> 00:08:36.860
In a paint shop, to compare the shades of two cans of paint, you set them side by side and weigh the difference with your eye.

91
00:08:36.860 --> 00:08:38.910
That is the human method.

92
00:08:38.910 --> 00:08:42.720
Now imagine you are holding a mold with twelve compartments.

93
00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:47.010
Each compartment is tuned to a particular range of color.

94
00:08:47.010 --> 00:08:51.940
When you hold a color up to the mold, whichever compartment lights up is the answer.

95
00:08:51.940 --> 00:08:56.870
You compare nothing with anything; you simply look at which compartment has been triggered.

96
00:08:56.870 --> 00:09:00.840
This method is fast and requires little computation.

97
00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:07.050
But it comes at a price: two similar colors that fall into the same compartment cannot be told apart.

98
00:09:07.050 --> 00:09:11.660
And that matches exactly the picture seen in the experiment.

99
00:09:11.660 --> 00:09:19.470
According to this idea, the twelve receptors exist not for fine discrimination, but for coarse yet very rapid classification.

100
00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:30.000
In the mantis shrimp's world, what really matters is not knowing the difference between two shades of orange, but grasping in an instant whether the thing in front of it is prey, a rival, or a mate.

101
00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:35.250
The reef is an environment where light shifts constantly with depth and with the murkiness of the water.

102
00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:41.300
Here, a fast and robust labeling system may prove more useful than a refined system of comparison.

103
00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:48.950
This needs to be said with a caveat: this explanation is a strong hypothesis consistent with the experimental results,

104
00:09:48.950 --> 00:09:55.000
but exactly how this recognition is carried out in the shrimp's nervous system has not yet been shown in detail.

105
00:09:55.000 --> 00:10:02.330
Some researchers also keep open the possibility that some comparison, however limited, may take place between the receptors.

106
00:10:02.330 --> 00:10:05.980
So how does the eye perform this recognition?

107
00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:10.110
This is where the narrowness of the midband comes into play.

108
00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:19.520
Because the band responsible for color vision consists of only a few rows of units, at any given moment the shrimp sees only a thin strip of its field of view in color.

109
00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:24.770
To gather the color information for the whole scene, it has to move its eyes.

110
00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:29.060
And that is exactly what mantis shrimp do.

111
00:10:29.060 --> 00:10:37.510
They pass their eyes over an object in slow, deliberate movements, sweeping the midband across the scene like the light bar of a scanner.

112
00:10:37.510 --> 00:10:44.360
You might picture the barcode reader at a supermarket checkout, or the light of a photocopier gliding beneath the glass.

113
00:10:44.360 --> 00:10:50.730
This is why Hanne Thoen and her colleagues call their proposed mechanism color recognition through temporal scanning.

114
00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:59.500
The color information is gathered over time as the eye moves, and each color is recognized by when, and how strongly, each receptor responds.

115
00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:04.590
This perspective also makes sense of another astonishing ability of the eye.

116
00:11:04.590 --> 00:11:10.640
The last two rows of the midband, as we mentioned earlier, detect the direction in which light vibrates.

117
00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:15.890
When light waves vibrate in a single plane, this is called linear polarization.

118
00:11:15.890 --> 00:11:21.460
Sunglasses that cut the glare reflecting off the surface of water work on this principle.

119
00:11:21.460 --> 00:11:25.110
Many animals can see linear polarization.

120
00:11:25.110 --> 00:11:28.440
But light can also be circularly polarized.

121
00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:37.850
In that case, the direction of vibration rotates like a corkscrew as the light travels forward, sometimes clockwise, sometimes counterclockwise.

122
00:11:37.850 --> 00:11:48.060
In two thousand eight, Tsyr-Huei Chiou, Justin Marshall and their colleagues showed that certain species of mantis shrimp can distinguish this circularly polarized light as well.

123
00:11:48.060 --> 00:11:53.470
It was the first time this ability had been documented so clearly anywhere in the animal kingdom.

124
00:11:53.470 --> 00:11:56.160
The mechanism is elegant.

125
00:11:56.160 --> 00:12:03.810
In the units of the last rows, a special cell creates a delay of a quarter of a wavelength between the two components of incoming light.

126
00:12:03.810 --> 00:12:10.340
This is exactly what the glass components known as quarter-wave plates do in physicists' optics laboratories.

127
00:12:10.340 --> 00:12:17.670
In this way, the rotating light is converted into light that vibrates in a straight line, which the cells beneath can detect.

128
00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:24.680
What's more, this biological plate performs consistently across a wider range of colors than many man-made plates.

129
00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:28.170
The position of the eye is no accident either.

130
00:12:28.170 --> 00:12:38.220
Like color information, polarization information is gathered through the scanning motion, and the shrimp can strengthen this signal by rotating their eyes around their own axes.

131
00:12:38.220 --> 00:12:41.550
This ability appears to serve a purpose.

132
00:12:41.550 --> 00:12:52.400
Certain regions on the bodies of some mantis shrimp species reflect circularly polarized light, and the animals display these regions during courtship or while defending their territory.

133
00:12:52.400 --> 00:12:58.450
This could mean they are able to communicate among themselves through a hidden channel that most predators cannot see.

134
00:12:58.450 --> 00:13:05.300
This interpretation, too, is supported by experiments, but it cannot yet be generalized to all species.

135
00:13:05.300 --> 00:13:11.030
Taken together, this picture shows that there is no single definition of what we call color vision.

136
00:13:11.030 --> 00:13:16.120
We see color with only a few receptors, but with powerful processing in the brain.

137
00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:27.130
The mantis shrimp, on the other hand, seems to have placed the burden on the eye itself: a large number of narrow-band receptors, a layered structure that separates them with filters,

138
00:13:27.130 --> 00:13:30.140
and a moving strip that scans the scene.

139
00:13:30.140 --> 00:13:36.830
Much of the computation is solved in the physics and movement of the eye, before the signal ever reaches the nervous system.

140
00:13:36.830 --> 00:13:44.320
And since the animal's brain is extremely small compared to ours, this trade-off looks like an evolutionarily sensible solution.

141
00:13:44.320 --> 00:13:48.770
This idea caught the attention of engineers as well.

142
00:13:48.770 --> 00:13:57.540
A sensor that makes fast decisions with little energy and little computation is exactly what is wanted for satellites, robots and small devices.

143
00:13:57.540 --> 00:14:02.150
Cameras inspired by the eye's polarization hardware are being developed.

144
00:14:02.150 --> 00:14:14.920
For example, at the University of Illinois, Viktor Gruev's team, working with Justin Marshall, developed a camera inspired by the mantis shrimp eye that records color and polarization at the same time.

145
00:14:14.920 --> 00:14:24.810
Researchers are investigating whether cameras like these, by revealing structural differences in tissue through polarization, could help identify certain cancerous tissues.

146
00:14:24.810 --> 00:14:31.500
These are not yet established tools in hospitals; they are promising, but ongoing, lines of research.

147
00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:38.510
Perhaps the most important thing this small animal teaches us is how misleading it can be to jump to conclusions by looking at numbers.

148
00:14:38.510 --> 00:14:43.280
Twelve receptors did not mean a world of color twelve times richer.

149
00:14:43.280 --> 00:14:47.410
They meant an entirely different answer to an entirely different question.

150
00:14:47.410 --> 00:14:52.660
We see colors by comparing them with one another, chasing after subtle differences.

151
00:14:52.660 --> 00:15:01.430
The mantis shrimp, by contrast, in the rapidly shifting light of the reef, sweeps its eye calmly across an object and recognizes it in an instant.

152
00:15:01.430 --> 00:15:04.920
Less detail, but more speed.

153
00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:10.810
Nature has solved the same problem not once, but many times, and differently each time.

154
00:15:10.810 --> 00:15:18.140
And to understand one of these solutions, the first thing needed was to question the animal not with prejudice, but with attention.

155
00:15:18.140 --> 00:15:21.790
We've come to the end of today's episode of Nöron Bilim.

156
00:15:21.790 --> 00:15:25.280
Until we meet again in a new story.
